{
  "$schema": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/primandproper/docent/main/schema/tour.v1.json",
  "docent": 1,
  "title": "How a step resolves",
  "description": "From a .tour file on disk to a highlighted band of code, in the order the data moves.",
  "isPrimary": true,
  "map": { "file": "how-a-step-resolves/map.mmd" },
  "steps": [
    {
      "id": "start",
      "title": "Start here",
      "description": "Docent is one binary with two faces. `docent [path]` opens a window; any subcommand runs headless.\n\nThis tour follows a single step of a tour from the JSON on disk to the highlighted lines you are looking at now. Both faces share that path, which is why `docent validate` and the app never disagree.\n\nPress `j` to go on."
    },
    {
      "id": "entry-point",
      "mapFocus": ["cli"],
      "title": "One entry point",
      "file": "main.go",
      "pattern": "^func run\\(\\) int",
      "line": 25,
      "description": "`main` hands everything to the CLI: the arguments, the output streams, and the built frontend, which is embedded in the binary a few lines up.\n\nNothing about the app is decided here. Whether a window opens is the root command's call."
    },
    {
      "id": "cli",
      "mapFocus": ["cli"],
      "title": "The cli package",
      "directory": "internal/cli",
      "description": "The root command opens the app. `validate` is the first headless command; the rest of the authoring commands will land beside it."
    },
    {
      "id": "parse",
      "mapFocus": ["file", "e1", "parse"],
      "title": "Parsing keeps what it doesn't understand",
      "file": "internal/tour/parse.go",
      "pattern": "^func Parse\\(",
      "line": 21,
      "description": "A tour is parsed twice over: into an ordered JSON tree that remembers key order and unknown fields, and from that tree into the typed model.\n\nSchema problems become diagnostics, not failures. A tour with a wrong field still opens, as far as it can be read."
    },
    {
      "id": "opt-in",
      "mapFocus": ["file", "e1", "parse"],
      "title": "Extensions are opt-in",
      "file": "internal/tour/parse.go",
      "pattern": "integer\\(doc, \"docent\"\\) == FormatVersion",
      "line": 54,
      "selection": { "start": { "line": 54, "character": 1 }, "end": { "line": 59, "character": 2 } },
      "description": "Docent's extension fields are only interpreted when the file sets `docent`. A plain CodeTour file that happens to use one of those names gets a warning and is otherwise left alone.\n\nNotice the call to `decodeTourExtensions`: that is the only way in. Press `j` to follow it."
    },
    {
      "id": "tour-extensions",
      "mapFocus": ["file", "e1", "parse"],
      "transition": "call",
      "title": "Reading the tour's own extensions",
      "file": "internal/tour/parse.go",
      "pattern": "^func decodeTourExtensions\\(",
      "line": 128,
      "selection": { "start": { "line": 128, "character": 1 }, "end": { "line": 161, "character": 2 } },
      "description": "Everything Docent adds at the top of a tour is read here: its repos, link variables, the system map, and who drafted and reviewed it.\n\nThis step sets `\"transition\": \"call\"`, which is why it opened out of the line that called it. Press `k` to watch it fold back."
    },
    {
      "id": "confine",
      "mapFocus": ["walker", "e3", "confine"],
      "title": "Reads are confined to the root",
      "file": "internal/gitsrc/worktree.go",
      "pattern": "^func Confine\\(",
      "line": 108,
      "description": "Tours are untrusted input, so every path in one is proven to stay inside its root before anything is read: once as written, and again after symlinks are followed.\n\nA path that escapes is reported as `path-escapes-root` and never touched."
    },
    {
      "id": "locate",
      "mapFocus": ["walker", "e4", "locate"],
      "title": "Locating the anchor",
      "file": "internal/anchor/anchor.go",
      "pattern": "^func Locate\\(",
      "line": 122,
      "description": "This is the whole of anchor resolution for a file that hasn't drifted:\n\n- `pattern` is matched per line. With `line`, the nearest match wins.\n- `selection` moves with its pattern.\n- `line` alone is absolute.\n\nThe result is a range plus a status. A step is never failed outright: Docent shows its best guess and says how far to trust it."
    },
    {
      "id": "walker",
      "mapFocus": ["parse", "e2", "walker"],
      "body": "how-a-step-resolves/07-walker.md",
      "title": "The walker ties them together",
      "file": "internal/walk/walk.go",
      "pattern": "^func \\(w \\*walker\\) Step\\(",
      "line": 213,
      "description": "The walker is the one place a step meets the code. The app's service and `docent validate` both call it, so there is no second implementation to drift."
    },
    {
      "id": "trust-boundary",
      "mapFocus": ["walker", "e5", "service"],
      "title": "The trust boundary",
      "file": "internal/app/service.go",
      "pattern": "^func \\(s \\*Service\\) Step\\(",
      "line": 595,
      "description": "The Wails service is deliberately thin. Its job is to distrust the frontend: a tour path must be one the workspace discovered, and a step index must be inside the tour, whatever the UI believed.\n\nFiles that are binary or very large are withheld here, with a reason, so they are never shipped to the webview."
    },
    {
      "id": "code-pane",
      "mapFocus": ["service", "e7", "pane"],
      "title": "Only the visible lines exist",
      "file": "frontend/src/components/CodePane.svelte",
      "pattern": "const LINE_HEIGHT = ",
      "line": 22,
      "description": "The code pane renders a fixed-height row per line and only the rows near the viewport, so a 20,000-line file scrolls like a short one.\n\nText appears immediately; Shiki's colors arrive a moment later, one lazily loaded grammar per language."
    },
    {
      "id": "next",
      "title": "Where to go next",
      "uri": "https://github.com/primandproper/docent/blob/main/docs/design.md",
      "description": "The design document covers what comes after this skeleton: several repos per tour, drift tracking from a stamped commit, the system map, and animated visuals."
    }
  ]
}
